d() → i915_drm_suspend(&i915->drm)` |\n\nChain detail (ground truth from source):\n\n```\ni915_pm_suspend(struct device *kdev)                     i915_driver.c:1392\n  │  i915 = kdev_to_i915(kdev)\n  │  if (i915->drm.switch_power_state == DRM_SWITCH_POWER_OFF) → return 0  (switcheroo off)\n  └─► i915_drm_suspend(&i915->drm)                       i915_driver.c:1392→1103\n          dev_priv  = to_i915(dev)\n          display   = dev_priv->display\n          ├─ disable_rpm_wakeref_asserts(&dev_priv->runtime_pm)\n          ├─ intel_power_domains_disable(display)\n          ├─ drm_client_dev_suspend(dev)\n          ├─ drm_kms_helper_poll_disable(dev) / intel_display_driver_disable_user_access(display)\n          ├─ intel_display_driver_suspend(display)   ← display suspend (see §3)\n          ├─ intel_irq_suspend(dev_priv), intel_hpd_cancel_work(display)\n          ├─ intel_display_driver_suspend_access(display), intel_encoder_suspend_all(display)\n          ├─ intel_dpt_suspend(display); i915_ggtt_suspend(to_gt(dev_priv)->ggtt)\n          ├─ i9xx_display_sr_save(display)\n          ├─ intel_opregion_suspend(display, PCI_D1|PCI_D3cold)\n          ├─ dev_priv->suspend_count++; intel_dmc_suspend(display)\n          ├─ enable_rpm_wakeref_asserts(...); i915_gem_drain_freed_objects(dev_priv)\n```\n\nThe *other* paths that also call `i915_drm_suspend` (not PCI PM callbacks, for completeness — `i915_drm_suspend` has exactly 3 direct callers in the graph):\n\n```\n.freeze = i915_pm_freeze                       i915_driver.c:1487 (PMSG_FREEZE/QUIESCE)\n  └─► i915_drm_suspend(&i915->drm)  (when switch_power_state != DRM_SWITCH_POWER_OFF)\n      then i915_gem_freeze(i915)\n\ni915_switcheroo_set_state()                    i915_switcheroo.c\n  └─► i915_driver_suspend_switcheroo()         i915_driver.c:1210\n      ├─► i915_drm_suspend(&i915->drm)\n      ├─► i915_drm_suspend_late(&i915->drm, false)\n      └─► pci_save_state(pdev); pci_set_power_state(pdev, PCI_D3hot)\n```\n\nThe remaining callbacks (`.suspend_late` → `i915_pm_suspend_late`, `.suspend_noirq` → `i915_pm_suspend_noirq`, `.poweroff_late/_noirq` → `i915_pm_poweroff_late/_noirq`) do **not** touch `i915_drm_suspend` — they only do the PCIe D-state / power-sequencing work (and `freeze_late` → `i915_pm_freeze_late` calls `i915_drm_suspend_late`, not `i915_drm_suspend`).\n\n# 2. Which of those callers also call `i915_pm_runtime_suspend`?\n\n**None — because `i915_pm_runtime_suspend` does not exist in this tree.** A whole-tree symbol search for `i915_pm_runtime_suspend` returns 0 hits (same for `i915_runtime_suspend`); that name is from the ~4.x-5.x i915, before runtime PM was restructured into `intel_runtime_pm`.\n\nIn this kernel the roles are split and verified via call graphs:\n\n- PCI PM (S3/S4) path: `i915_pm_suspend`/`i915_pm_freeze`/`i915_driver_suspend_switcheroo` → `i915_drm_suspend` + `i915_drm_suspend_late` (direct callers of `i915_drm_suspend_late`: `i915_pm_suspend_late`, `i915_pm_freeze_late`, `i915_pm_poweroff_late`, `i915_driver_suspend_switcheroo`) — none of them calls any `..._runtime_suspend` wrapper; they go straight to the PCI layer.\n- Runtime PM (S0ix) path: `i915_pm_ops.runtime_suspend = intel_runtime_suspend` (i915_driver.c:1752), which is **mutually exclusive** with the PCI PM path — its callees are `intel_display_power_suspend`, `intel_gt_runtime_suspend`, `i915_gem_runtime_suspend`, `intel_uncore_suspend`, `intel_irq_suspend` etc. It never passes through `i915_drm_suspend` or `intel_display_driver_suspend`.\n\n# 3. Data flow of `dev_priv` from `i915_drm_suspend` down to display suspend\n\nNote: in this tree the display-suspend function is **`intel_display_driver_suspend(struct intel_display *)`** (`display/intel_display_driver.c:684`) — the name `intel_display_suspend` no longer exists. Flow of the private data:\n\n```\ni915_pm_suspend(struct device *kdev)\n    i915 (struct drm_i915_private *) = kdev_to_i915(kdev)     ← container_of via to_drm_i915\n    i915_drm_suspend(&i915->drm)                              ← passes struct drm_device *\n\ni915_drm_suspend(struct drm_device *dev)                      i915_driver.c:1103\n    dev_priv = to_i915(dev)        →  struct drm_i915_private * (container_of, i915_drv.h)\n    display  = dev_priv->display   →  struct intel_display * (field of drm_i915_private)\n\n        └─► intel_display_driver_suspend(display)             display/intel_display_driver.c:684\n                display->drm                          ← reverse backlink to struct drm_device\n                state = drm_atomic_helper_suspend(display->drm)\n                display->restore.modeset_state = state\n                flush_workqueue(display->wq.cleanup)\n                intel_dp_mst_suspend(display)\n\n        (still using dev_priv directly for the non-display parts:)\n    intel_irq_suspend(dev_priv)\n    i915_ggtt_suspend(to_gt(dev_priv)->ggtt)\n    suspend_to_idle(dev_priv) → opregion PCI_D1 vs PCI_D3cold\n    dev_priv->suspend_count++\n    disable/enable_rpm_wakeref_asserts(&dev_priv->runtime_pm)\n    i915_gem_drain_freed_objects(dev_priv)\n```\n\nSo the object handed down is `dev_priv` (`drm_i915_private`) converted one level: `to_i915(dev)` recovers it from `drm_device`, then `dev_priv->display` extracts the `intel_display` sub-object, which is what `intel_display_driver_suspend` receives; the `display->drm` back-pointer is used inside it to reach the core DRM APIs (`drm_atomic_helper_suspend`). The `dev_priv`-specific operations (IRQ, GGTT, opregion, GEM drain) stay in `i915_drm_suspend` itself."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"ollama","model":"qwen3.8:27b-q8_0","usage":{"input":19946,"output":1932,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":21878,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1787746561304,"responseId":"chatcmpl-563","rawStopReason":"stop"}],"willRetry":false}
